## Range Access with Slices

A **slice** gives you a view into a contiguous range of a Vec without copying data. Use range syntax to create slices.

### Creating Slices

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(10); v.push(20); v.push(30); v.push(40); v.push(50);

let slice = v.slice(1, 4);  // elements at indices 1, 2, 3: [20, 30, 40]
```

`.slice(start, end)` includes `start` but excludes `end` — just like range notation.

### Slice Ranges

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(1); v.push(2); v.push(3); v.push(4); v.push(5);

let first_three = v.slice(0, 3);  // [1, 2, 3]
let last_two = v.slice(3, 5);     // [4, 5]
let all = v.slice(0, v.len());    // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
let empty_slice = v.slice(2, 2);  // [] — start == end
```

### Processing Slices

```xiom
fn sum_slice(v: &Vec[Int], start: Int, end: Int) -> Int {
  let slice = v.slice(start, end);
  var total = 0;
  for item in slice {
    total = total + item;
  }
  return total;
}
```

### Slice as Function Parameter

```xiom
fn process_range(v: &Vec[Int], range_start: Int, range_end: Int) -> Int {
  let subset = v.slice(range_start, range_end);
  var max = subset[0];
  var i = 0;
  while i < subset.len() {
    if subset[i] > max { max = subset[i]; }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return max;
}
```

### Slice Independence

A slice is a view — modifying the original Vec might invalidate it. Use slices for read-only operations.

```xiom
let data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1); data.push(2); data.push(3);
let view = data.slice(0, 2);  // view of [1, 2]
// Use view for reading — don't modify data while view is in use
```

